  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 158 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nQuite a bit of uncertainty remains with this upcoming storm\nsystem. A few clear trends appear to be emerging, mixed across the\nnoise of a full suite of 12z CAMs and global/ensemble solutions.\n\nA well defined upper level low continues to spin near the CA/NV/AZ\nborder this afternoon. Meanwhile, Hurricane Polo made its second\nlandfall, this time over northwestern Mexico, earlier today and\ncontinues to race northeastward thanks to the steering flow of the\naforementioned closed low. The upper low should continue drifting\neastward over the next 48 hours, with the associated vorticity\nfrom the remnants of Polo slinging around the east/northeastern\nperiphery of the low on Wednesday. Deep moisture is expected to\nlift northward tonight into early Wednesday, with a precipitation\nshield developing over the southern half of the state. That\nprecipitation should crawl northward during the day, before\neventually pushing off to the east as the closed low slowly starts\nto shear out by Wednesday night.\n\nMany folks will not be surprised by today's forecast \ndevelopments... with a pronounced drying trend noted across most \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance since this time yesterday. As \nis often the case with closed lows south of the Four Corners \nregion, it's rather difficult to get well aligned moisture/upslope\nthis far north. There remain a handful of holdouts across today's\nguidance... most notably a few MPAS-core CAMS, a few REFS members,\nand the(soon to be retired) NAM and 3km NAM, which continue to\nsuggest at the potential for significant rainfall totals as far\nnorth as the Denver metro. Conversely, there are now more members\nof the GEFS and ECMWF ensemble that have zero precipitation for\nDEN (11, with several more with <0.05\") than those with >1\" of QPF\n(8). \n\nWhile those CAMs have been bullish regarding the mid-level low \nand surface cyclogenesis in northern New Mexico/southeastern \nColorado, I am rather skeptical of these solutions. These seem to\nbe dependent on a convectively driven feedback cycle that results\nin a much stronger/westerly track of the surface low. While\npossible, this feels implausible. Indeed, even comparing near \nterm HRRR forecasts, it seems likely that there will be a \ncontinued correction to the southeast with it's QPF. Though we\ncan't completely ignore the wetter solutions, it's also difficult\nto ignore a rather clear signal from the rest of 12z guidance \nthat favors the weaker 700mb and surface low. Unsurprisingly, we \ndid nudge lower PoPs and QPF down a bit, though this likely wasn't\nnearly enough of a shift and I suspect the subsequent forecast \nwill see more dramatic cuts there. We also trimmed the extent of \nour Flood Watch, which now only covers far southeastern Elbert and\nsouthern Lincoln counties.\n\nAfter this storm system departs, guidance is in good agreement\nthat a broad ridge will develop over the Rockies by the end of\nthis week. Outside of a weak shortwave or two working through the\nnorthwest flow aloft, this should lead to an extended period of\nnear/above normal temperatures and little if any precipitation\nthrough at least early next week, if not a bit longer than that.\nMOS guidance suggests highs near 80F from Friday through most of\nnext week, with some deterministic guidance a bit warmer.",
